TTT2 project patch 0001 for the ReXGlue SDK (pin c94f5eb, v0.10.0): fall-through at a function's end.

When a function's recorded extent (its .pdata length, a [functions] size, a gap-fill run) ends before its
code does, ReXGlue ends the generated C++ function there, so it returns to the caller where the CPU goes on
into the code that follows - silently, without a warning. Code skipped that way is lost: a lock that is
never left, a stack frame and non-volatile registers that are never restored.
recomp/tests/ppc/falloff_ttt2.s measures it: a function whose .pdata length ends after it enters a
critical section leaves the section held after every call (recursion 5 after 5 calls; the owner's boots
show a section held by the main thread at recursion 390 while two other threads wait for it).

With this patch the generated code continues where the CPU does: a jump to the label when the code that
follows belongs to the same function, otherwise a call of the function that starts there followed by a
return (a tail call: LR is untouched, so that function's blr returns to the original caller). Every site
is logged ("Fall-through after <mnemonic>: function 0x... runs on past its end at 0x... into ...") and
marked in the generated code ("// TTT2 fall-through after ..."); recomp/tools/codegen_report.py counts
them. Where no function starts at that address the generated code still returns (logged and marked).
The codegen command also names the patch in its log ("TTT2 SDK patches: 0001-fall-through") so the
pipeline can tell an SDK package built without it.

Applied by scripts/fetch-rexglue-sdk.sh / .ps1. The SDK is BSD-3-Clause (third_party/rexglue-sdk/PIN).

diff --git a/src/codegen/function_graph.cpp b/src/codegen/function_graph.cpp
index fc918bb..db75e8a 100644
--- a/src/codegen/function_graph.cpp
+++ b/src/codegen/function_graph.cpp
@@ -344,6 +344,38 @@ void emit_print(std::string& out, fmt::format_string<Args...> fmt, Args&&... arg
   fmt::vformat_to(std::back_inserter(out), fmt.get(), fmt::make_format_args(args...));
 }
 
+// Whether the CPU can go on to the next word after this instruction: everything but an unconditional
+// b/ba, blr, bctr (branch-always BO, no link) and an unconditional trap. A zero or undecodable word is
+// data, never treated as falling through.
+bool CanFallThrough(uint32_t raw) {
+  if (raw == 0 || raw == 0xFFFFFFFF)
+    return false;
+  const uint32_t op = raw >> 26;
+  const bool link = (raw & 1) != 0;
+  const uint32_t bo = (raw >> 21) & 31;
+  const bool always = (bo & 0x14) == 0x14;
+  switch (op) {
+    case 18:  // b, ba, bl, bla
+      return link;
+    case 16:  // bc
+      return link || !always;
+    case 19: {
+      const uint32_t xo = (raw >> 1) & 0x3FF;
+      if (xo == 16 || xo == 528)  // bclr, bcctr
+        return link || !always;
+      return true;
+    }
+    case 3:  // twi
+      return bo != 31;
+    case 31:
+      if (((raw >> 1) & 0x3FF) == 4)  // tw
+        return bo != 31;
+      return true;
+    default:
+      return true;
+  }
+}
+
